WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.133
Welcome to Module 4 of the
Microsoft Automation learning path:

2
00:00:04.133 --> 00:00:08.138
Azure Arc through Azure Resource
Manager and Resource Graph.

3
00:00:08.138 --> 00:00:13.295
The Federal Modernization Workbook's
Devices and Endpoints book puts the agency's

4
00:00:13.295 --> 00:00:16.435
servers under Azure Arc,
and its Data Platform book

5
00:00:16.435 --> 00:00:19.949
uses Arc to bring SQL Server
under Microsoft Entra I D.

6
00:00:19.949 --> 00:00:23.113
After onboarding, the
question becomes evidence.

7
00:00:23.113 --> 00:00:25.388
None of that is in Microsoft Graph.

8
00:00:25.388 --> 00:00:27.277
Arc servers, their extensions,

9
00:00:27.277 --> 00:00:32.265
SQL Server instances and Azure Policy
are Azure resources, managed through

10
00:00:32.265 --> 00:00:36.649
Azure Resource Manager and read in
bulk through Azure Resource Graph.

11
00:00:36.649 --> 00:00:40.800
In this module you'll learn what
changes when a script leaves Graph for

12
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:45.450
Resource Manager, and how to read Resource
Graph without silently losing rows.

13
00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:47.862
Then you'll run five read-only checks,

14
00:00:47.862 --> 00:00:51.400
in Python and PowerShell,
against a fictional estate.

15
00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:54.609
Like the other modules'
checks, they're illustrative:

16
00:00:54.609 --> 00:00:59.290
unit-tested against fixtures, not
proven against live subscriptions.

17
00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:04.190
Why does this matter?

18
00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:08.802
Once the servers are under Arc, an assessor
asks questions a script can answer.

19
00:01:08.802 --> 00:01:12.552
Which servers lack the monitoring
and endpoint protection agents?

20
00:01:12.552 --> 00:01:15.302
Which still reach Azure
over the public internet?

21
00:01:15.302 --> 00:01:19.990
Which run an operating system or SQL
Server version past the end of support?

22
00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:23.290
Is each SQL Server instance's
host still connected?

23
00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:27.428
And which policy assignments are failing,
and which aren't enforcing anything?

24
00:01:27.428 --> 00:01:32.864
The validation kit already has seven
Arc-track checks on Resource Graph: tags,

25
00:01:32.864 --> 00:01:36.346
agent status, baseline
compliance, CMDB coverage,

26
00:01:36.346 --> 00:01:40.678
Update Manager, role assignments,
and two tools on one setting.

27
00:01:40.678 --> 00:01:42.528
This module doesn't repeat them.

28
00:01:42.528 --> 00:01:44.328
Its checks read other records.

29
00:01:44.328 --> 00:01:46.748
One more thing frames the module:

30
00:01:46.748 --> 00:01:52.395
the workbook plans on Windows Server
2016 being gone from production by its

31
00:01:52.395 --> 00:01:57.864
January twelfth, 2027 end of support,
with Windows Server 2019 the minimum.

32
00:01:57.864 --> 00:02:02.430
That's the program's planning
assumption, not a federal requirement,

33
00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:06.050
and any 2016 server that
remains is an exception.

34
00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:12.633
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

35
00:02:12.633 --> 00:02:15.337
which takes about ninety-five
minutes with the lab.

36
00:02:15.337 --> 00:02:20.000
You'll explain the four ways Resource
Manager differs from Graph for a script.

37
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:22.764
You'll page a Resource
Graph query to the end,

38
00:02:22.764 --> 00:02:26.987
and say when Resource Graph can't
page and why that must stop the run.

39
00:02:26.987 --> 00:02:31.725
You'll explain why an empty result can
mean no access, not a clean estate.

40
00:02:31.725 --> 00:02:35.890
You'll read the Arc machine,
extension, SQL Server instance,

41
00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:40.300
policy assignment and policy state
records, and the traps in each.

42
00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:44.773
And you'll say which Windows Server and
SQL Server versions are past support,

43
00:02:44.773 --> 00:02:46.738
and run the five checks on fixtures.

44
00:02:46.738 --> 00:02:48.049
You need Module 1.

45
00:02:48.049 --> 00:02:51.475
Lab 2 needs a test subscription
in commercial Azure,

46
00:02:51.475 --> 00:02:55.030
never production, with a
few Arc-enabled servers.

47
00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:02.880
A script that already calls Graph
correctly has four things to change.

48
00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:05.605
First, another host and another token.

49
00:03:05.605 --> 00:03:11.013
Resource Manager is management dot azure
dot com in commercial Azure, and a GCC

50
00:03:11.013 --> 00:03:16.255
tenant's subscriptions are commercial
Azure, so a GCC script calls that host.

51
00:03:16.255 --> 00:03:22.992
GCC High and DoD use Azure Government,
management dot us gov cloud api dot net.

52
00:03:22.992 --> 00:03:28.479
A token is issued for one resource, so a
Graph token can't call Resource Manager.

53
00:03:28.479 --> 00:03:32.868
Second, Azure role-based access
control, not a permission list.

54
00:03:32.868 --> 00:03:35.974
A Resource Manager token
carries no roles list;

55
00:03:35.974 --> 00:03:39.318
Azure decides at each call
from the roles assigned.

56
00:03:39.318 --> 00:03:44.492
Every call here needs only the built-in
Reader role, whose only action is read.

57
00:03:44.492 --> 00:03:50.055
Third, an api-version on every
call, set by each resource provider.

58
00:03:50.055 --> 00:03:53.493
Fourth, paging: next link,
not the O Data next link,

59
00:03:53.493 --> 00:03:56.755
and Resource Graph
pages differently again.

60
00:03:56.755 --> 00:04:00.311
The five checks make just two
calls, and the Resource Graph

61
00:04:00.311 --> 00:04:04.140
query is a POST only because
the query travels in the body.

62
00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:11.905
Resource Graph holds a copy of
every resource's properties,

63
00:04:11.905 --> 00:04:15.265
in tables you query with
the Kusto Query Language.

64
00:04:15.265 --> 00:04:16.652
It has four traps.

65
00:04:16.652 --> 00:04:19.665
One: an empty result can mean no access.

66
00:04:19.665 --> 00:04:22.234
Microsoft Learn says no results come back

67
00:04:22.234 --> 00:04:24.803
for objects you can't
read, without an error.

68
00:04:24.803 --> 00:04:28.484
So every check reports machines
in scope and empty scope,

69
00:04:28.484 --> 00:04:31.070
the client refuses a query with no scope,

70
00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:35.614
and the command line stops when the
identity can see no subscriptions.

71
00:04:35.614 --> 00:04:38.327
Two: paging is a token in the body.

72
00:04:38.327 --> 00:04:40.814
A page holds at most a thousand records;

73
00:04:40.814 --> 00:04:44.853
repeat the same query with the
skip token until none comes back.

74
00:04:44.853 --> 00:04:49.904
Three: some queries can't be paged at
all, when they use limit, sample or take,

75
00:04:49.904 --> 00:04:54.365
or when every output column is
dynamic, as project properties is.

76
00:04:54.365 --> 00:04:58.703
Then the client raises an incomplete
result rather than return part of one.

77
00:04:58.703 --> 00:05:04.302
Four: sort by a unique column, or pages
can repeat some records and skip others.

78
00:05:04.302 --> 00:05:06.777
Every query ends ordered by I D.

79
00:05:06.777 --> 00:05:09.189
And the data isn't strongly consistent,

80
00:05:09.189 --> 00:05:12.630
so a check run minutes after
a change may not see it.

81
00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:18.005
Unit 4 is the records.

82
00:05:18.005 --> 00:05:22.749
The Arc machine record carries its
status, connected, disconnected,

83
00:05:22.749 --> 00:05:28.172
error or awaiting connection; its operating
system type, name, S K U and version;

84
00:05:28.172 --> 00:05:32.662
the private link scope it's assigned
to, if any; and its E S U profile,

85
00:05:32.662 --> 00:05:36.305
with eligibility and whether
a license is assigned.

86
00:05:36.305 --> 00:05:40.850
Each extension is its own resource,
whose I D begins with the machine's,

87
00:05:40.850 --> 00:05:43.880
which is how Microsoft's
own sample joins them.

88
00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:48.155
The check reads the publisher, type,
version and provisioning state.

89
00:05:48.155 --> 00:05:53.449
By default it requires two: Azure Monitor
Agent, for log and performance collection,

90
00:05:53.449 --> 00:05:58.130
and Microsoft Defender for Endpoint,
which Defender for Servers deploys.

91
00:05:58.130 --> 00:06:02.125
A SQL Server instance enabled
by Arc carries its version,

92
00:06:02.125 --> 00:06:05.880
edition and status, and the I
D of the machine that hosts it.

93
00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:10.393
One rule matters more than the rest:
never read an extension's settings.

94
00:06:10.393 --> 00:06:14.176
They're public JSON, and Microsoft's
example is a command line,

95
00:06:14.176 --> 00:06:18.450
so a collector that reads them
copies commands into the evidence.

96
00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:25.088
Unit 5 covers policy and lifecycle.

97
00:06:25.088 --> 00:06:29.400
A policy assignment's enforcement mode
decides whether its effect applies.

98
00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:30.862
Default enforces it.

99
00:06:30.862 --> 00:06:35.673
Do not enforce still evaluates and
reports compliance, but prevents nothing:

100
00:06:35.673 --> 00:06:37.853
the right way to stage a deny policy,

101
00:06:37.853 --> 00:06:42.888
but one left there reports noncompliance
forever, so each needs a dated decision.

102
00:06:42.888 --> 00:06:46.188
Enroll enforces only
for enrolled resources.

103
00:06:46.188 --> 00:06:50.782
Running a version past its end of
support is NIST special publication

104
00:06:50.782 --> 00:06:55.613
eight hundred fifty-three's S A
twenty-two, unsupported system components.

105
00:06:55.613 --> 00:06:58.574
The dates come from
Microsoft's lifecycle pages.

106
00:06:58.574 --> 00:07:04.337
Windows Server 2012 is unsupported:
its extended security updates ended on

107
00:07:04.337 --> 00:07:10.100
October thirteenth, 2026, and Arc E S U
licenses were deactivated the next day.

108
00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:15.765
Windows Server 2016's extended support
ends January twelfth, 2027, and under the

109
00:07:15.765 --> 00:07:21.688
workbook's server standard, one still in
production after that date is an exception.

110
00:07:21.688 --> 00:07:29.738
SQL Server 2012 is unsupported, and
2014 and 2016 are in their E S U years.

111
00:07:29.738 --> 00:07:34.160
Where two Learn pages disagree,
the kit uses the earlier date.

112
00:07:37.910 --> 00:07:38.960
Five checks.

113
00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:42.535
Arc extensions lists servers
missing Azure Monitor Agent

114
00:07:42.535 --> 00:07:46.110
or Defender for Endpoint, and
extensions that didn't deploy.

115
00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:49.818
Arc private link lists servers
with no private link scope,

116
00:07:49.818 --> 00:07:52.522
so they reach Azure over public endpoints.

117
00:07:52.522 --> 00:07:58.171
Lifecycle sorts servers and SQL Server
instances by support state: unsupported,

118
00:07:58.171 --> 00:08:00.995
E S U not confirmed, E S
U covered, ending soon,

119
00:08:00.995 --> 00:08:05.434
and products not in the table, which
are never counted as supported.

120
00:08:05.434 --> 00:08:08.999
SQL inventory lists instances
by version and edition,

121
00:08:08.999 --> 00:08:12.860
and the ones that are disconnected
or whose host is out of scope.

122
00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:17.735
And policy summary shows each assignment's
enforcement mode and compliance counts.

123
00:08:17.735 --> 00:08:21.441
Every control they evidence is
in the NIST Moderate baseline;

124
00:08:21.441 --> 00:08:25.147
the mapping is the program's
reading, and the assessor decides.

125
00:08:25.147 --> 00:08:27.023
All five need only Reader.

126
00:08:27.023 --> 00:08:31.380
Two limits to keep in mind: an
extension that succeeded isn't proof the

127
00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:35.585
agent reports, and a private link
scope isn't the whole network path.

128
00:08:35.585 --> 00:08:38.300
Confirm those for a sample of servers.

129
00:08:42.050 --> 00:08:42.775
Try it.

130
00:08:42.775 --> 00:08:47.734
Lab 1 runs each check against the kit's
fictional estate: one subscription

131
00:08:47.734 --> 00:08:53.263
with seven Arc servers, five SQL Server
instances and four policy assignments.

132
00:08:53.263 --> 00:08:57.205
The from-json option evaluates
saved Resource Graph rows

133
00:08:57.205 --> 00:09:01.312
instead of calling Azure, and
as-of fixes the evaluation date.

134
00:09:01.312 --> 00:09:03.462
Here's step three, lifecycle.

135
00:09:03.462 --> 00:09:04.787
Two are unsupported.

136
00:09:04.787 --> 00:09:08.545
S R V L E G zero one runs
Windows Server 2012 R2,

137
00:09:08.545 --> 00:09:13.762
and it has an Arc E S U license
assigned, but the E S U years ended

138
00:09:13.762 --> 00:09:19.501
on October thirteenth, 2026, so no
updates come through any channel.

139
00:09:19.501 --> 00:09:22.450
And a SQL Server 2012 instance.

140
00:09:22.450 --> 00:09:26.218
Two SQL Server instances, 2014 and 2016,

141
00:09:26.218 --> 00:09:31.063
are in their E S U years with
no confirmed subscription.

142
00:09:31.063 --> 00:09:35.829
And S R V A P P zero two runs
Windows Server 2016, ending soon:

143
00:09:35.829 --> 00:09:39.885
the case the workbook's
server standard is about,

144
00:09:39.885 --> 00:09:45.362
upgraded before January twelfth,
2027, or a documented exception.

145
00:09:45.362 --> 00:09:50.924
Step seven asks for a query with no scope,
and the client stops before any request.

146
00:09:50.924 --> 00:09:53.663
The lab repeats two checks in PowerShell,

147
00:09:53.663 --> 00:09:57.490
with the same results, and
twenty-four Arc tests pass.

148
00:10:01.240 --> 00:10:05.420
Lab 2 runs the checks read-only
against your own test subscription

149
00:10:05.420 --> 00:10:09.752
in commercial Azure, never production,
with a few Arc-enabled servers.

150
00:10:09.752 --> 00:10:14.031
One Azure C L I command registers the
app with a self-signed certificate

151
00:10:14.031 --> 00:10:17.802
and no password, and assigns it
Reader on the test subscription.

152
00:10:17.802 --> 00:10:22.156
No Graph permission is needed, and
never assign Contributor or Owner,

153
00:10:22.156 --> 00:10:24.753
which can change every resource in scope.

154
00:10:24.753 --> 00:10:28.777
Confirm the app holds one
role, Reader, and nothing else.

155
00:10:28.777 --> 00:10:32.264
Then run each check, in
Python or PowerShell.

156
00:10:32.264 --> 00:10:36.811
If machines in scope is zero and you
know the subscription has Arc servers,

157
00:10:36.811 --> 00:10:40.240
the role hasn't taken effect
yet, or it's on the wrong scope.

158
00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:45.172
Record two open review items: what
operating system S K U your servers report,

159
00:10:45.172 --> 00:10:49.165
and whether Reader alone can
call Policy Insights' summarize.

160
00:10:49.165 --> 00:10:54.890
Compare each result with the portal's Arc,
extension, SQL Server and Policy views.

161
00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:59.246
Then back it out: remove the role,
delete the app and its certificate,

162
00:10:59.246 --> 00:11:03.840
and delete the saved results, which
hold server names and resource I Ds.

163
00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:06.340
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

164
00:11:10.090 --> 00:11:11.402
Where are agencies?

165
00:11:11.402 --> 00:11:14.499
The agency in the Devices
and Endpoints book is

166
00:11:14.499 --> 00:11:17.991
onboarding Windows and Linux
servers to Arc in waves.

167
00:11:17.991 --> 00:11:21.745
During the move, some servers are
onboarded but missing an agent,

168
00:11:21.745 --> 00:11:26.351
some connect over the public endpoints
before the private link scope is ready,

169
00:11:26.351 --> 00:11:31.027
and Arc makes end-of-support Windows
Server and SQL Server versions visible.

170
00:11:31.027 --> 00:11:35.255
A GCC Moderate estate's subscriptions
are in commercial Azure,

171
00:11:35.255 --> 00:11:39.807
so its scripts call management dot
azure dot com and the commercial

172
00:11:39.807 --> 00:11:43.790
sign-in endpoint; use the G C C
cloud option, as in every module.

173
00:11:43.790 --> 00:11:46.178
The interim paths are design guidance.

174
00:11:46.178 --> 00:11:51.199
Servers onboarded before the private link
scope exists are findings with a target

175
00:11:51.199 --> 00:11:53.673
date; assign the policy that requires the

176
00:11:53.673 --> 00:11:56.366
scope in do-not-enforce
first, then enforce it.

177
00:11:56.366 --> 00:12:00.753
Windows Server 2012 servers
still running are a red finding.

178
00:12:00.753 --> 00:12:07.063
And each Windows Server 2016 server is
upgraded to 2019 or later before January

179
00:12:07.063 --> 00:12:11.748
twelfth, 2027, or becomes an
exception, with E S Us through Arc,

180
00:12:11.748 --> 00:12:15.840
a signed risk acceptance
and a retirement date.

181
00:12:19.590 --> 00:12:22.931
GCC High and DoD estates
use Azure Government:

182
00:12:22.931 --> 00:12:28.383
management dot us gov cloud api dot net,
the US Government sign-in authority,

183
00:12:28.383 --> 00:12:32.428
and the Azure US Government
environment in PowerShell.

184
00:12:32.428 --> 00:12:38.295
Microsoft's Azure Government availability
roadmap, read on November eleventh, 2026,

185
00:12:38.295 --> 00:12:42.487
shows Resource Manager, Resource
Graph, Arc-enabled servers,

186
00:12:42.487 --> 00:12:46.343
Azure Policy and Private
Link generally available and

187
00:12:46.343 --> 00:12:50.702
authorized at FedRAMP High and
DoD impact levels four and five.

188
00:12:50.702 --> 00:12:54.300
Arc-enabled SQL Server is
available at FedRAMP High,

189
00:12:54.300 --> 00:12:57.115
and awaiting review at
levels four and five.

190
00:12:57.115 --> 00:12:59.090
Three differences affect the checks.

191
00:12:59.090 --> 00:13:04.654
SQL Server enabled by Arc is a subset
there: the US Gov Virginia region only, on

192
00:13:04.654 --> 00:13:07.689
Windows only, with no
Entra authentication,

193
00:13:07.689 --> 00:13:10.977
so SQL inventory sees
only those instances.

194
00:13:10.977 --> 00:13:14.828
The private link scope is
available, with its own endpoints.

195
00:13:14.828 --> 00:13:18.653
And E S U-only Arc servers need
a smaller set of endpoints.

196
00:13:18.653 --> 00:13:21.869
Learn doesn't say whether
Resource Graph's quota,

197
00:13:21.869 --> 00:13:26.420
paging or tables differ in Azure
Government: not yet specified.

198
00:13:30.170 --> 00:13:31.557
The federal requirement.

199
00:13:31.557 --> 00:13:36.529
Every control the checks evidence is in
the NIST special publication eight hundred

200
00:13:36.529 --> 00:13:40.364
fifty-three, revision five, Moderate
baseline: system monitoring,

201
00:13:40.364 --> 00:13:44.838
malicious code protection and audit
record generation, through the agents;

202
00:13:44.838 --> 00:13:47.608
boundary protection,
through the network path;

203
00:13:47.608 --> 00:13:52.295
unsupported system components and flaw
remediation; the component inventory;

204
00:13:52.295 --> 00:13:55.633
and continuous monitoring
and configuration settings.

205
00:13:55.633 --> 00:13:59.820
The mapping is the program's reading;
the agency's assessor decides.

206
00:13:59.820 --> 00:14:03.410
The evidence an ISSO keeps:
each check's dated output,

207
00:14:03.410 --> 00:14:06.582
with its evidences list
and machines in scope;

208
00:14:06.582 --> 00:14:09.754
the app's Reader role
assignment and its scope;

209
00:14:09.754 --> 00:14:13.678
a decision for each unsupported
server, migrate, upgrade,

210
00:14:13.678 --> 00:14:15.765
or isolate and accept the risk;

211
00:14:15.765 --> 00:14:21.191
the E S U subscription record for each
instance whose coverage isn't confirmed;

212
00:14:21.191 --> 00:14:26.617
the dated decision behind each do-not-enforce
assignment; and for each Windows

213
00:14:26.617 --> 00:14:30.958
Server 2016 server still in
production after January twelfth,

214
00:14:30.958 --> 00:14:32.795
2027, the exception record.

215
00:14:32.795 --> 00:14:37.360
The Arc track implementation guide's
Step 8 puts this on a schedule.

216
00:14:41.110 --> 00:14:43.085
Check yourself with two questions.

217
00:14:43.085 --> 00:14:47.023
First: a Resource Graph
query returns no Arc servers.

218
00:14:47.023 --> 00:14:48.636
What's the first thing to check?

219
00:14:48.636 --> 00:14:53.363
The answer is b: whether the identity
has Reader on the scope, because Resource

220
00:14:53.363 --> 00:14:57.647
Graph returns nothing for resources
the caller can't read, and no error.

221
00:14:57.647 --> 00:15:03.934
Second: a Windows Server 2012 R2 machine
has an Arc E S U license assigned.

222
00:15:03.934 --> 00:15:08.010
What does lifecycle report
on November third, 2026?

223
00:15:08.010 --> 00:15:10.247
The answer is b, unsupported.

224
00:15:10.247 --> 00:15:14.060
The E S U years ended on
October thirteenth, 2026,

225
00:15:14.060 --> 00:15:18.361
so no updates come through
any channel, license or not.

226
00:15:18.361 --> 00:15:23.620
The module page has three more questions,
each linked to the unit that answers it.

227
00:15:27.370 --> 00:15:28.195
To sum up.

228
00:15:28.195 --> 00:15:32.840
You learned what changes when a script
leaves Graph for Resource Manager:

229
00:15:32.840 --> 00:15:36.342
another host and token, Azure
role-based access control

230
00:15:36.342 --> 00:15:39.845
instead of a permission list,
api-versions, and next link.

231
00:15:39.845 --> 00:15:42.773
You paged Resource Graph
without losing rows,

232
00:15:42.773 --> 00:15:46.933
and saw when it can't page and why
an empty result can mean no access.

233
00:15:46.933 --> 00:15:51.996
You read the Arc machine, extension,
SQL Server, policy assignment and policy

234
00:15:51.996 --> 00:15:56.983
state records, and checked support dates
against Microsoft's lifecycle pages.

235
00:15:56.983 --> 00:16:01.416
And in Lab 1 you ran the five checks
on fixtures in Python and PowerShell,

236
00:16:01.416 --> 00:16:04.045
and read each finding against its control.

237
00:16:04.045 --> 00:16:08.318
That finishes the Microsoft Automation
learning path as it stands:

238
00:16:08.318 --> 00:16:12.057
four modules, about five hours
and fifty-five minutes in all.

239
00:16:12.057 --> 00:16:15.394
To put the checks on a schedule,
go to the Azure Arc track

240
00:16:15.394 --> 00:16:18.657
implementation guide's Step
8, and its Gate 8 checks it.

241
00:16:18.657 --> 00:16:21.705
Modules on Teams Phone
and Teams administration,

242
00:16:21.705 --> 00:16:24.440
and on monitoring and
evidence, are planned.
